  "before_strategy_sha256": "d1e3bb1c6f461b6a3f8adaab4586d10450fd899d1218d55a9905e6b17e16f5cf",
  "after_strategy": "1. At rank zero use the convention in the definition. At one strand there are no crossings, so the presentation gives exactly the displayed truncated polynomial ring and homogeneous monomial basis. For positive $\\lambda_i$ the unique graded simple is its degree-zero quotient and evaluation identifies the regular cover's projective Hom dual with itself. At $\\lambda_i=0$, $1=0$ and there is no simple. The nonzero trace input is in degree $2d_i(\\lambda_i-1)$ and its output in degree zero, giving the NEGATIVE functional degree; the anti-diagonal monomial pairing proves perfectness over every field.\n2. The explicit nil-Hecke endomorphism supplier gives $NH_n=\\operatorname{End}_{S_n}(P_n)$ with $S_n=k[e_1,\\ldots,e_n]$, $P_n=k[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]$. To compute the first-power ideal, use the finite free intermediate algebra $C_n=S_n[z]/(z^n-e_1z^{n-1}+\\cdots+(-1)^ne_n)$, embedded by $z=x_1$. Polynomial division of $\\prod_a(t-x_a)$ by $t-x_1$ identifies $C_n$ with $k[x_1,e'_1,\\ldots,e'_{n-1}]$ for the remaining variables. Their Artin basis makes $P_n$ free of rank $(n-1)!$ over $C_n$. Thus multiplication by $x_1^{\\lambda_i}$ is repeated multiplication by $z^{\\lambda_i}$ on the $S_n$-basis $1,z,\\ldots,z^{n-1}$. The two-sided matrix ideal it generates is the matrix ideal of its entries.\nUse the coefficient trace $\\epsilon(z^{n-1})=1$, $\\epsilon(z^a)=0$ for $a<n-1$. The power-basis trace matrix is anti-triangular with unit anti-diagonal, so its dual basis is integral over $S_n$. Its recurrence from the displayed monic equation gives $\\epsilon(z^{n-1+r})=h_r$. Accordingly the ideal of multiplication entries equals the ideal of all $\\epsilon(z^{\\lambda_i+a+b})=h_{\\lambda_i+a+b-n+1}$ for $0\\le a,b<n$, taking $h_r=0$ for $r<0$ and $h_0=1$. The order-$n$ recurrence expresses all indices above $\\lambda_i$ in the first consecutive $n$ entries $h_{\\lambda_i-n+1},\\ldots,h_{\\lambda_i}$, giving exactly the stated corner ideal. For $n>\\lambda_i$ it contains $h_0=1$; for $n\\le\\lambda_i$ all these generators have positive degree and the ideal lies in the augmentation ideal, so its quotient is NONZERO. It is finite by the independently proved finite-block supplier and the matrix corner, with degree-zero part $k$ and nilpotent positive-degree ideal. It consequently has one normalized graded simple. No Schur-basis or unproved Grassmannian-cohomology identification is needed.\n3. The actual nil-Hecke matrix equivalence therefore gives exactly one simple column for every nonzero block. Its augmentation column is the ordinary $S_n$ coinvariant polynomial representation, with its Artin monomial basis and centering shift $-d_i\\binom n2$. Its graded dimension is $q_i^{-\\binom n2}\\prod_{r=1}^n(1+q_i^2+\\cdots+q_i^{2(r-1)})=[n]_{q_i}!$. The generic divided-power/normalized-simple supplier identifies this column as self-dual; quotient inflation preserves that normalization. Together with the rank-zero class, this gives precisely $\\lambda_i+1$ classes, not a conclusion based solely on comparing a module dimension.\n4. At $n=\\lambda_i=2$ the symmetric corner is $k$. The column is the stated two-dimensional coinvariant module; $x_2=-x_1$, $x_1^2=0$, and multiplication/divided difference give the matrix units even in characteristic two. The regular endomorphism algebra is the column tensored with its vector dual, yielding the two shifts $\\pm d_i$ and preserving the exact $[2]_{q_i}$ regular multiplicity. A one-dimensional zero-dot proposal violates the native equal-color mixed relation and is impossible. At $\\lambda_i=1,n=2$ one can also see vanishing directly: from $x_1=0$ the actual native mixed equations give $x_2\\tau=-1$ and $\\tau x_2=-1$; multiplying by $\\tau$ and using $\\tau^2=0$ gives $\\tau=0$ and then $1=0$.\n5. Finally the coprime monic factors $u,u-1$ give $k\\oplus k$, with two ordinary simple classes. This proves the deformation boundary while retaining the full standard graded rank-one scope.",
